<section><div class="wrap"><div class="prose" style="max-width:820px">
<span class="eyebrow">Custom kitchens</span>
<h2>A kitchen designed for your house, not picked from a catalogue</h2>
<p>A custom kitchen starts with the room and the people who use it. Where the light comes from, where the plumbing and gas can go, how many people cook at once, whether the island is for prep, for eating or both, and how much storage the household really needs. The cabinetry is then drawn to fit those answers, down to the filler pieces, the panel ends and the height of the toe kick.</p>
<p>In Toronto that often means working with an older house: a narrow semi or detached home where the kitchen is closed off from the dining room, the walls may be load-bearing, and the ventilation and electrical were never planned for today&rsquo;s appliances. Opening the room up is often possible, but it needs a structural solution and usually a building permit, which we prepare in-house with our <a href="/services/permit-drawings-toronto/">BCIN permit drawings</a>.</p>
<p>In new custom homes in Vaughan and across the GTA, the kitchen is usually designed together with the rest of the house, so the island lines up with the windows, the tall cabinets line up with the ceiling details, and the materials carry through to the dining room and family room.</p>
</div></div></section>

<section style="background:var(--card);border-block:1px solid var(--line)"><div class="wrap">
<div class="sec-head"><div><span class="eyebrow">What is included</span>
<h2>Every detail planned before it is built</h2></div></div>
<ol class="svc">
<li><span class="n">01</span><h3>Layout</h3><p>Work zones, island and seating, clearances and circulation planned around how you cook.</p></li>
<li><span class="n">02</span><h3>Cabinetry and millwork</h3><p>Cabinets, pantry and built-ins designed in detail, so a cabinet maker can price and build them accurately.</p></li>
<li><span class="n">03</span><h3>Materials and finishes</h3><p>Counters, backsplash, flooring, hardware and colours brought together on one concept board.</p></li>
<li><span class="n">04</span><h3>Appliances and lighting</h3><p>Appliance specs and clearances checked before ordering, with layered lighting for cooking and evenings.</p></li>
<li><span class="n">05</span><h3>3D renders</h3><p>You see the finished kitchen before anything is ordered or demolished.</p></li>
<li><span class="n">06</span><h3>Installation on site</h3><p>We follow the installation so the kitchen is built the way it was designed.</p></li>
</ol>
</div></section>

<section><div class="wrap">
<div class="sec-head"><div><span class="eyebrow">Process</span>
<h2>How a kitchen design project works</h2></div></div>
<ol class="steps">
<li><h3>Consultation</h3><p>We look at your space, talk through how you use the kitchen, your budget and timing, and whether the work will need a permit. The first consultation is free.</p></li>
<li><h3>Layout and concept</h3><p>We measure, propose layout options, and bring colours, materials and finishes together on a concept board for you to approve.</p></li>
<li><h3>Renders and drawings</h3><p>3D renders show the finished room. Plans, elevations and cabinet details give your contractor and cabinet maker the same clear job to price.</p></li>
<li><h3>Installation</h3><p>We answer questions from site and check the installation against the design, from the cabinet layout to the last panel.</p></li>
</ol>
</div></section>

<section style="padding-top:0"><div class="wrap">
<div class="sec-head"><div><span class="eyebrow">Layouts</span>
<h2>Kitchen layouts we design</h2></div></div>
<div class="layouts">
<div><h3>Island kitchens</h3><p>An island for prep, seating or both, sized so there is room to open the dishwasher and the oven with people walking past.</p></div>
<div><h3>Open-concept kitchens</h3><p>Kitchens opened to the dining and family room, often by removing or replacing a wall, with the cabinetry finished to be seen from the living space.</p></div>
<div><h3>L-shaped and U-shaped kitchens</h3><p>Efficient layouts for semis and narrower rooms, with corners planned so storage is reachable instead of lost.</p></div>
<div><h3>Galley kitchens</h3><p>Two parallel runs for long, narrow rooms, common in Toronto row houses and condos, where every centimetre of cabinetry counts.</p></div>
</div>
</div></section>

